    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Own a memory-efficient scalar candidate. Replace the O(S*N^2) serpentine scan with a separable rolling box sum: for W=N-S+1, form each row’s W horizontal S-sums, retain S such rows in a circular buffer, update W vertical totals, and reduce only after row S-1. This is O(N^2) with O(S*W+N) storage. Use a robust buffered signed-integer reader, int64 accumulators, and a maximum initialized from a real window. Validate negative matrices, S=1, S=N-1, and arbitrary whitespace; report whole-program timings and memory to G.\",\"B\":\"Own an independent 4-core candidate. Read into one flat row-major int matrix, partition the W output-start columns into contiguous bands, and give each persistent worker a band plus its S-1 right halo. Each worker scans rows, maintains int64 vertical sums by subtracting row r-S, then rolls horizontally to obtain its local maximum; reduce local maxima at the end. Adapt worker count when halo duplication or small W makes four workers wasteful. Avoid per-row thread creation, locks, false sharing, and a second full int64 image. Include parsing/allocation in measurements; do not require a changed toolchain merely to parallelize.\",\"C\":\"Own a distinct low-memory/vector-friendly scalar route plus I/O research. Keep a circular buffer of S raw input rows and length-N int64 vertical column totals; as each row arrives, replace the outgoing raw row, update independent column totals, then roll horizontally across those totals once S rows are available. This is also O(N^2) but has different locality from A. Compare fused parse/update with row-buffered, vectorizable updates and provide a reusable fast-reader result. Retain only portable, measured ARM64-friendly optimizations; preserve EOF/whitespace and all-negative correctness.\",\"G\":\"Integrate empirically, not mechanically. First verify every tree candidate against the unchanged oracle and boundary semantics, including int64 intermediate arithmetic and exact one-line output. Start from the fastest complete valid program; transplant one isolated idea at a time (for example, a reader or layout improvement) and benchmark full runs repeatedly on the same harness. The baseline is correct at 0.131956 s mean, with noticeable sample variation, so require credible end-to-end gains. A combined implementation may be slower or use too much memory; keep the fastest valid scalar solution if threading loses. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The only measurement is the correct baseline (0.131956 s mean; samples 0.127599–0.137127 s). Its sliding updates touch S values per move, yielding O(S*N^2), while jagged storage and mixed formatted input add avoidable overhead. The primary opportunity is an exact O(N^2) separable SxS sum; parsing/layout are next, and ARM64’s four cores are worth testing but may lose at this short runtime. The final result fits int32, but valid intermediate sums should use int64. No candidate measurements exist yet, so no approach should be assumed superior or specialized to the four observed correctness cases.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"0aba362cc8a594f73eea750262be270d2c7be95182aac04579b2d25e3abec0ce\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Own a memory-efficient scalar candidate. Replace the O(S*N^2) serpentine scan with a separable rolling box sum: for W=N-S+1, form each row’s W horizontal S-sums, retain S such rows in a circular buffer, update W vertical totals, and reduce only after row S-1. This is O(N^2) with O(S*W+N) storage. Use a robust buffered signed-integer reader, int64 accumulators, and a maximum initialized from a real window. Validate negative matrices, S=1, S=N-1, and arbitrary whitespace; report whole-program timings and memory to G.\",\"B\":\"Own an independent 4-core candidate. Read into one flat row-major int matrix, partition the W output-start columns into contiguous bands, and give each persistent worker a band plus its S-1 right halo. Each worker scans rows, maintains int64 vertical sums by subtracting row r-S, then rolls horizontally to obtain its local maximum; reduce local maxima at the end. Adapt worker count when halo duplication or small W makes four workers wasteful. Avoid per-row thread creation, locks, false sharing, and a second full int64 image. Include parsing/allocation in measurements; do not require a changed toolchain merely to parallelize.\",\"C\":\"Own a distinct low-memory/vector-friendly scalar route plus I/O research. Keep a circular buffer of S raw input rows and length-N int64 vertical column totals; as each row arrives, replace the outgoing raw row, update independent column totals, then roll horizontally across those totals once S rows are available. This is also O(N^2) but has different locality from A. Compare fused parse/update with row-buffered, vectorizable updates and provide a reusable fast-reader result. Retain only portable, measured ARM64-friendly optimizations; preserve EOF/whitespace and all-negative correctness.\",\"G\":\"Integrate empirically, not mechanically. First verify every tree candidate against the unchanged oracle and boundary semantics, including int64 intermediate arithmetic and exact one-line output. Start from the fastest complete valid program; transplant one isolated idea at a time (for example, a reader or layout improvement) and benchmark full runs repeatedly on the same harness. The baseline is correct at 0.131956 s mean, with noticeable sample variation, so require credible end-to-end gains. A combined implementation may be slower or use too much memory; keep the fastest valid scalar solution if threading loses. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The only measurement is the correct baseline (0.131956 s mean; samples 0.127599–0.137127 s). Its sliding updates touch S values per move, yielding O(S*N^2), while jagged storage and mixed formatted input add avoidable overhead. The primary opportunity is an exact O(N^2) separable SxS sum; parsing/layout are next, and ARM64’s four cores are worth testing but may lose at this short runtime. The final result fits int32, but valid intermediate sums should use int64. No candidate measurements exist yet, so no approach should be assumed superior or specialized to the four observed correctness cases.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"nonce\":\"cebd910f8abf46428912b16f2774b81a\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"0aba362cc8a594f73eea750262be270d2c7be95182aac04579b2d25e3abec0ce\",\"session_id\":\"15219a6fd62b4dcb91b395dcd4ae03d5\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"93a7058c9315e87ccaf5ec37058a8f8da550db4bb0031a188ba78a936a5079f5\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":9695,\"output_tokens\":17158,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":16491}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":26853,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
